  "title": "SYSTEM AND METHOD FOR PRESENTATION OF COMPUTER VISION USING USER DATA",
  "technical_field": "[0002] This disclosure relates generally to systems and methods for presenting computer vision content (e.g., augmented reality, virtual reality). More specifically, this disclosure relates to systems and methods for generating computer vision content (e.g., augmented reality content) using user data.",
  "background_art": "[0003] Computer vision applications allow users to view digital information overlaid onto real-world environments. For example, a user may use a mobile device camera to capture video or stills of their surroundings while receiving additional information about those surroundings via the mobile device's screen. Such computer vision applications often rely upon specific visual cues (e.g., markers, codes, etc.) being present within the captured media. [0004] However, these visual cues can limit how much information is presented to a user at any given time. In some cases, the user must actively select a particular marker or other cue in order to receive relevant information. As such, there exists a need for improved computer vision solutions.",
  "summary_of_invention": "[0005] Systems and methods described herein provide for presentation of computer vision content (e.g., augmented reality) using user data. According to certain embodiments, a client device can identify features (e.g., scannable codes) in one or more images (e.g., images captured using the client device's camera(s)). These features can correspond to user accounts associated with respective users. Accordingly, the client device can access data corresponding to these user accounts to generate computer vision content (e.g., augmented reality displays). For instance, if the identified features correspond to a user account of a friend of the current user, the client device can retrieve posts made by the friend and generate animated virtual objects (e.g., \"bubbles\") containing text from these posts. The animated virtual objects can appear as though they are floating out of the identified features. Thus, a user does not have to scan individual scannable codes to see what his/her friends have posted. Rather, the user can simply look around them and watch the animations pop up from nearby features. [0006] Certain aspects of the technology described herein relate to detecting features in images. Features can take many forms, including but not limited to QR codes, barcodes, NFT tokens, NFC tags, AR markers, etc. Once detected, the features can be analyzed to determine whether they contain identifiers (e.g., URLs, IP addresses, hashes, etc.). If so, the features can be matched against known databases of identifiers to identify which features match which user accounts. In some implementations, the matching process involves reverse hashing or similar techniques to identify matches between features and user accounts. [0007] When a feature is identified as belonging to a particular user account, the client device can access data stored in association with the user account. This data can include social media posts, chat logs, messages, emails, texts, etc. From this data, the client device can extract items of interest (e.g., text, audio, video, images, etc.), such as comments made by the user, responses to comments, likes, shares, etc. The extracted items can be used to populate virtual objects (e.g., bubbles), which can be displayed as overlays to real-world images. In some examples, the virtual objects can be generated as animations, allowing them to move and change appearance over time. [0008] Virtual objects can be configured to animate in various ways depending on the type of item of interest. For example, different colors, shapes, sizes, patterns, etc. can be assigned to different types of items. Different animation sequences can also be defined for different types of items. For instance, a comment might trigger a bubble that floats upwards from the surface it was detected near, whereas a like might trigger a smaller bubble that bounces off the surface before disappearing. Other behaviors can be programmed into the virtual objects as well. [0009] To make the experience even more immersive, the client device can analyze the environment surrounding the detected feature. For example, the client device can recognize surfaces, textures, lighting conditions, etc. Based on this analysis, the client device can adjust the appearance of the virtual objects accordingly. For example, bright colors could be chosen for lighted areas and muted tones for darkened spaces. Similarly, texture can influence the shape or pattern of the virtual objects. [0010] Additionally, the client device can track movement of the user relative to the feature. This tracking allows the client device to position the virtual objects in relation to the user's point of view. For instance, if the user moves closer to a feature, the virtual objects will appear larger. Conversely, if the user backs away, the virtual objects will shrink. By adjusting the size of the virtual objects according to distance, the client device can create a sense of depth and scale. [0011] In some implementations, the client device can combine multiple pieces of information to generate the virtual objects. For example, the client device can consider both the type of item of interest and its location when determining how to animate the virtual object. Using this combination approach, the client device can create highly personalized experiences tailored to the user's interests and context. [0012] Another aspect of the technology described herein relates to providing feedback mechanisms for users who interact with the virtual objects. For instance, a user can click on a virtual object to open a menu with options for interacting with the underlying item of interest. Options can include actions such as liking, commenting, sharing, saving, etc. Some options may require authentication prior to execution. In addition to menus, the client device can also employ other interfaces to facilitate interaction. For example, voice commands can be recognized to control playback of the virtual objects. Gestures can also be tracked to initiate interactions. Furthermore, the client device can utilize haptic feedback to simulate tactile sensations, making the experience feel more engaging and interactive. [0013] In another aspect, the technology described herein provides for enhanced security measures to protect sensitive information. Since the virtual objects are tied directly to physical locations, unauthorized access becomes difficult without knowledge of the physical space. Moreover, because the virtual objects only exist within the vicinity of the original feature, they cannot be easily replicated or shared outside of that area. This limits the spread of potentially malicious content and ensures that users remain connected to trusted sources. [0014] Another aspect of the technology described herein relates creating and managing user accounts. Users can register new accounts through the client device itself, whereupon they can upload personal details and preferences. Existing accounts can be integrated with existing social networks, messaging platforms, email services, etc. This integration enables seamless login capabilities and synchronization across all platforms. Upon registration, users can set permissions regarding which data is accessible to whom. They can choose to share their own data freely, restrict access to certain individuals or groups, or opt-out entirely. [0015] In terms of managing existing accounts, users can modify settings related to their profile information, notification preferences, privacy controls, etc. They can also delete their account altogether, removing all associated data from the server-side database. Additionally, administrators can manage user accounts en masse, handling tasks such as moderating content, enforcing community guidelines, and monitoring activity levels. This administrative functionality helps maintain a safe and welcoming online environment. [0016] Another aspect of the technology described herein relates generating and displaying augmented reality content. For example, a client device can detect features (e.g., scannable codes, AR markers, etc.) in one or more images. The client device can then identify user accounts associated with the detected features. In response, the client device can retrieve data from the user accounts and generate augmented reality displays (e.g., virtual objects, animations, etc.) based on this data. [0017] Augmented reality displays can take many forms, ranging from simple icons to complex simulations. Each form can convey unique information and engage users differently. For instance, icons can represent basic status updates, while animations can depict dynamic events unfolding in real-time. Simulations, meanwhile, can immerse users in fully-realized scenarios, blurring lines between the digital and physical worlds. [0018] In some instances, augmented reality displays can be designed to react dynamically to changing circumstances. For example, a simulation might adapt to environmental factors such as weather, time of day, temperature, etc. It can also respond to user inputs, incorporating gestures, speech, biometric data, etc. By integrating these variables, the augmented reality displays become more nuanced and responsive, offering a richer experience. [0019] Another aspect of the technology described herein relates enhancing collaboration among users. For example, multiple clients devices can communicate with each other to synchronize augmented reality displays. This coordination enables simultaneous viewing of the same content across multiple devices, fostering group engagement and discussion. In some instances, the synchronized displays can be adjusted according to user proximity. For instance, as two users draw closer together, their displays can begin to mirror each other, reinforcing a sense of connection. As they separate, however, their displays diverge once again, reflecting their individual perspectives. [0020] In another aspect, the technology described herein facilitates remote collaboration. Through cloud-based servers, users can contribute to projects regardless of geographical separation. Real-time communication tools enable seamless exchange of ideas and work-in-progress. Version history and revision management ensure transparency and accountability throughout the creative process. Collaboration can occur within designated channels, ensuring focused discussions and streamlined workflow. Notifications keep participants informed of changes and progress. [0021] Another aspect of the technology described herein relates facilitating e-commerce transactions. For instance, augmented reality displays can be used to showcase products in three dimensions. Users can manipulate the product virtually, rotating it, zooming in/out, examining different angles, etc. Prices, reviews, and other product information can be accessed through hovering effects or tapping on the product. Secure payment gateways integrate seamlessly with popular wallets, enabling swift checkout processes. In some cases, loyalty programs and rewards schemes can be implemented, incentivizing repeat business. [0022] In another aspect, the technology described herein enhances experiential marketing efforts. Brands can leverage augmented reality displays to tell compelling stories, convey values, and build emotional connections with customers. Interactive campaigns encourage participation and engagement, fostering brand loyalty. Targeted advertising strategies reach consumers in meaningful contexts, increasing effectiveness. Social media integrations amplify campaign reach and impact. Campaign analytics provide insights into consumer behavior, informing future marketing decisions. [0023] Another aspect of the technology described herein relates improving navigation and wayfinding. Clients can use augmented reality displays to visualize directions, distances, and estimated travel times. Indoor mapping technologies help navigate complex buildings and spaces. Points of Interest (POIs) can be highlighted, providing contextual information about landmarks, shops, restaurants, etc. Users can save favorite POIs for later reference. Waypoints can be created and shared for collaborative exploration. Navigation assistance can be offered for visually impaired users, utilizing audio descriptions and voice guidance. [0024] In another aspect, the technology described herein improves accessibility features. For instance, augmented reality displays can assist individuals with disabilities. Visual impairments can be accommodated through audio narrations, Braille outputs, high contrast modes, etc. Hearing-impaired users can benefit from closed captions, sign language interpretation, and vibration alerts. Physical limitations can be addressed through adaptive layouts, simplified interfaces, and gesture recognition. Cognitive challenges can be mitigated through clear typography, minimal clutter, and customizable font sizes. Accessibility guides and tutorials can educate users on optimal usage. Feedback mechanisms allow continuous improvement of accessibility features. [0025] Yet another aspect of the technology described herein relates to safety enhancements. For instance, emergency situations can be handled through panic buttons, crisis hotlines, and alert systems. Personal safety apps can monitor users' whereabouts and notify contacts in case of danger. First aid resources and self-defense tips can be provided during emergencies. Crime prevention advice and awareness campaigns can be disseminated. Emergency contact numbers and procedures can be readily available. Automated reporting tools streamline incident documentation. Safety training modules offer educational content. [0026] Another aspect of the technology described herein pertains to health and wellness support. For example, mental health resources and counseling services can be accessible through augmented reality displays. Mood-tracking tools and stress-reducing exercises can be suggested. Fitness classes and workout routines can be guided by AI-powered coaches. Nutrition planning and meal suggestions can be offered based on dietary needs. Sleep schedules and habits can be monitored, with recommendations for improvement. Medication reminders and dosage trackers can be managed. Health records and medical histories can be securely stored. Telemedicine consultations can be facilitated remotely. [0027] In yet another aspect, the technology described herein relates to education enhancement. For instance, interactive lessons can be delivered through augmented reality displays. Gamification mechanics can motivate learning and retention. Educational materials can be customized to individual students' needs and abilities. Language barriers can be overcome through translation tools and subtitles. Accessible formats for visually impaired learners can be provided. Collaborative learning tools enable peer-to-peer teaching and discussion. Virtual field trips and remote explorations can expand curricula. Academic research and project resources can be organized and referenced. Learning analytics can inform educators about student performance. [0028] Another aspect of the technology described herein concerns entertainment offerings. For instance, games can be played in augmented reality, leveraging spatial reasoning and problem-solving skills. Immersive storytelling experiences can transport viewers to fantastical realms. Live concerts and performances can be attended remotely, with real-time commentary and Q&A sessions. Sports events can be watched from diverse viewpoints, with statistics and play-by-play analysis. Historical reenactments and cultural festivals can be brought to life. Cultural artifacts and historical relics can be explored in 360-degree detail. Art exhibitions and galleries can be curated digitally. Music composition and creation tools can be provided. [0029] In another aspect, the technology described herein relates to tourism and travel facilitation. For instance, destination guides can be accessed through augmented reality displays. Local attractions and hidden gems can be revealed. Travel plans can be coordinated and booked through integrated booking engines. Maps and GPS navigation can be optimized for unfamiliar territories. Cultural immersion activities and workshops can be recommended. Multilingual support can be provided for international travelers. Safety advisories and local laws can be communicated. Accommodation bookings and reservations can be managed. Currency conversion rates and exchange services can be offered. [0030] Another aspect of the technology described herein relates financial management. For instance, budgeting and expense tracking tools can be provided. Investment portfolios and market analysis can be accessed. Cryptocurrency trading and wallet management can be supported. Credit card statements and transaction history can be viewed. Bill payments and invoices can be sent and received. Tax preparation and filing assistance can be offered. Financial news and market trends can be updated regularly. Retirement savings and investment planning can be advised. Insurance policies and quotes can be compared. [0031] In another aspect, the technology described herein concerns customer service improvements. For instance, automated chatbots can handle routine inquiries and issues. Human representatives can be reached for complex problems. Product returns and exchanges can be processed efficiently. Order tracking and shipping updates can be provided. Refund requests and cancellations can be submitted. Account balances and rewards points can be checked. Loyalty program benefits and offers can be redeemed. Support documents and FAQs can be accessed. [0032] Another aspect of the technology described herein pertai ns to transportation optimization. For instance, route-planning algorithms can optimize routes based on traffic, road closures, and construction. Public transit schedules and timetables can be synced with calendar appointments. Parking availability and pricing can be found. Ride-hailing services can be summoned and paid for. Traffic cameras and sensors can provide real-time congestion reports. Roadside assistance and towing services can be dispatched. Fuel prices and gas station locations can be searched. Carpooling and ride-sharing opportunities can be matched. Vehicle maintenance and repair estimates can be obtained."
}

