  "technical_field": "[0002] This relates generally to artificial reality systems, such as augmented reality (AR) or mixed reality (MXR) systems, and more particularly to automatic generation of boundaries for virtual reality (VR) experiences.",
  "background_art": "[0003] Virtual Reality (VR) refers to a computer-generated simulation of a three-dimensional image or environment that simulates physical presence within it.
[0004] In contrast, Augmented Reality (AR) refers to a live direct or indirect, interactive superimposition of a virtual object into a real-world scene, typically through a device with a camera or other sensor(s).
[0005] Mixed Reality (MXR) may combine elements of both AR and VR to create an immersive experience that blends digital information with the physical world.
[0006] To enable these technologies, users often wear head-mounted displays (HMDs), such as goggles or glasses, which provide a field of vision displaying the simulated environment. HMDs are also equipped with sensors that detect the user's movements and position them in the simulated environment accordingly.
[0007] One challenge associated with providing MXR experiences is ensuring accurate tracking and localization of the HMD in the real-world environment. Localization involves accurately mapping the location of the HMD in relation to its surroundings, allowing the system to render the correct perspective and positioning of virtual objects in the real-world environment.
[0008] Real-time tracking and localization require continuous monitoring of the HMD's position and orientation in the real-world environment. This enables the system to maintain consistency between the virtual and real environments, preventing disorientation and improving overall immersion.
[0009] However, this continuous tracking comes with some challenges. For example, environmental factors like lighting changes, occlusions, and reflections can negatively impact tracking accuracy. Additionally, maintaining smooth performance requires significant computational resources, potentially affecting battery life and power consumption.
[0010] Moreover, the complexity of handling different types of surfaces and features in the real-world environment can lead to difficulties in achieving robust and reliable tracking. For instance, certain materials or textures might not be easily recognizable by the tracking system, resulting in reduced accuracy or even loss of tracking altogether.
[0011] Furthermore, large open spaces or areas with limited distinguishing features can pose challenges for establishing initial anchor points necessary for tracking. Similarly, navigating complex indoor spaces, such as those with multiple levels or irregularly shaped rooms, demands sophisticated algorithms capable of adapting to diverse layouts.
[0012] Another challenge lies in dealing with dynamic environments, where temporary obstructions or moving objects can interfere with tracking capabilities. This necessitates efficient methods for recalibrating and adjusting the tracking system to accommodate changing conditions.
[0013] These challenges highlight the need for advanced techniques that address the intricacies of real-world environments and improve the reliability and efficiency of tracking and localization in MXR applications.",
  "summary_of_invention": "[0022] Various aspects of the technology described herein relate to creating a boundary for a virtual reality (VR) experience. Such a boundary defines an area in the real-world space where the VR experience will be rendered. Creating a suitable boundary for the VR experience is important because it ensures the VR content appears properly aligned with the real-world environment, thereby enhancing the realism and immersiveness of the VR experience. If the boundary is too small, VR content may appear disconnected from the real-world environment, reducing the sense of immersion. On the other hand, if the boundary is too large, it may consume unnecessary CPU/GPU resources and increase latency due to excessive calculations needed to track the real-world environment. Therefore, there is a need for automated methods and systems that efficiently determine a suitable boundary for a VR experience.

[0032] In accordance with various aspects of the present disclosure, an artificial reality system, while rendering an AR/MXR environment, can scan and gather visual characteristic data about a user's real-world environment in the background.
[0033] Upon detecting an intent to transition to VR mode (e.g., by launching a new VR app), the artificial reality system generates a recommended boundary for the real-world space. This recommended boundary can take into account various factors, such as the size and shape of the room, the layout of furniture, and the presence of obstacles or other relevant features. The artificial reality system can use the gathered visual characteristic data to inform the recommended boundary. The artificial reality system then presents the user with the recommended boundary, along with options to accept, modify, or reject it.
[0034] Once the user has selected their preferred boundary, the artificial reality system begins rendering the VR environment within that defined area. During this time, the artificial reality system continues to monitor the real-world space and updates the boundary as needed. This ongoing monitoring allows the artificial reality system to adapt to any changes in the real-world environment, such as new furniture placement or rearrangement of existing items.
[0035] By automating the boundary selection process, the artificial reality system streamlines the setup process for VR experiences, saving users time and effort. It also reduces errors caused by incorrect boundary settings, leading to improved overall user satisfaction. Furthermore, this approach enhances the quality of the VR experience by ensuring that the virtual environment aligns correctly with the real-world surroundings, thus increasing immersion and engagement.
[0036] In addition, by continuously updating the boundary, the artificial reality system adapts to evolving circumstances, making it easier for users to enjoy seamless transitions between AR/MXR and VR modes.
[0037] According to another aspect of the present disclo- sure, an artificial reality system can determine whether the generated boundary covers enough of the real-world space. If the generated boundary does not cover enough of the real-world space, the artificial reality system prompts the user to continue scanning the real-world space until the generated boundary covers enough of the real-world environment.
[0038] In yet another aspect of the present disclosure, the artificial reality system determines an appropriate interaction mode for a VR experience based on the generated boundary. Interaction modes refer to how the user interacts with the virtual environment during the VR experience. Two common interaction modes include "moveable" and "stationary," where moveable means the user can walk around in the virtual environment, whereas stationary restricts the user to remain in place within the virtual environment. Determining an appropriate interaction mode depends on several factors, including the size of the generated boundary. For example, if the boundary is relatively small compared to the real-world space, the artificial reality system may recommend a stationary interaction mode to prevent the user from accidentally walking out of the designated boundary. Conversely, if the boundary is larger, the artificial reality system may suggest a moveable interaction mode, allowing the user to explore the entire virtual environment freely. The user can choose to override the suggested interaction mode if desired.
[0039] In still another aspect of the present disclosure, the artificial intelligence system provides feedback regarding the generated boundary. This feedback can occur before entering VR mode, giving the user the opportunity to review and approve or modify the proposed boundary. The user interface for presenting the boundary can vary depending on the specific implementation but may include graphical representations of the real-world space, highlighting the recommended boundary, along with controls to accept, reject, or edit the boundary. By offering this level of control, users have flexibility in customizing their VR experience according to their preferences and needs.
[0040] Additional aspects of the present disclosure involve implementing the above-described functionality in hardware and software components. For example, the artificial reality system can comprise one or more central processing units (CPUs) and memory devices configured to store programs and data structures that implement the disclosed functionalities. The memory devices can be volatile or non-volatile, fixed or removable, and can include hard disks, solid-state drives, flash drives, RAM, ROM, etc. The CPUs can be coupled to the memory devices via buses or other communication links. The artificial reality system can also include graphics processing units (GPUs) dedicated to rendering the virtual environment. Further, the artificial reality system can incorporate various sensors, such as cameras, gyroscopes, accelerometers, magnetometers, ultrasonic distance sensors, light sensors, microphones, global navigation satellite system (GNSS) receivers, inertial measurement units (IMU), and proximity sensors, among others, to facilitate tracking and localization.
[0041] While the foregoing description focuses primarily on AR/MXR systems, similar principles apply to MR systems. MR combines elements of AR and VR to create an immersive experience that blurs the lines between the digital and physical worlds. MR uses a mix of digital content and real-world elements to create an interactive environment. Similar to AR, MR relies on tracking and localization capabilities to ensure precise alignment of digital assets with real-world objects and spaces. MR experiences often utilize markerless tracking, leveraging cameras and machine-learning-based algorithms to identify patterns and features in the real-world environment, enabling accurate spatial understanding and registration of digital content.
[0042] In general, MR experiences can benefit from the same automation of boundary creation as discussed above. Thus, an MR system can similarly scan a real-world space and generate a boundary for an MR experience, taking into account the unique requirements of MR applications. For example, MR experiences may rely heavily on the ability to recognize and interact with real-world objects, so the boundary must encompass sufficient context to allow for proper integration of digital content with physical elements. By automating the boundary creation process, MR developers can focus on crafting engaging experiences rather than spending time setting up the underlying infrastructure.
[0043] In addition, MR systems can leverage similar approaches to handle cases where the generated boundary does not adequately capture the real-world space. This could involve prompting the user to expand the boundary, update the tracking system, or employ alternative tracking mechanisms to ensure stable and accurate localization throughout the MR experience.
[0044] Furthermore, MR systems can implement similar tech- niques to determine optimal interaction modes for each experi- ence. Depending on the nature of the MR experience, the system may recommend different interaction modes to enhance user engagement. For instance, a puzzle game might suggest a stationary mode to encourage players to examine details closely, while an educational tour might recommend a moveable mode to facilitate exploration of the surrounding environment.
[0045] Overall, the present disclosure addresses key chal- lenges in the development and delivery of AR/MRX/VR expe- riences, focusing on optimizing the creation and management of boundaries in real-world spaces. By automating the process, the disclosed solutions streamline workflows, reduce potential errors, and enhance overall user satisfaction across various artificial reality platforms.",
